{:edb/db-before-t 1249 :edb/db-after-t 1250 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534562 50 #inst "2026-10-08T01:04:51.127Z" 13194139534562 true] [17592186087390 1100 "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = 0$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = x^4$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = y$" 13194139534562 true] [17592186087390 1100 "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n1. $y' + x^3 y = 0$\n2. $y' + x^3 y = x^4$\n3. $y' + x^3 y = y$" 13194139534562 false] [17592186087390 1145 "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = 0\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n$y' + x^3 y = 0$.\nNotice that this has the form $y' + P(x)y = 0$. Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n- Next, we consider the equation\n$y' + x^3 y = x^4$.\nNotice that this is written in the form $y' + P(x)y = Q(x)$ where $Q(x) = x^4$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n$y' + x^3 y = y$.\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n$y^{\\prime}+x^3y-y=0$, or $y^{\\prime}+(x^3-1)y=0$.\nSince our equation can be written in the form $y' + P(x)y = 0$, the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534562 false] [17592186087390 1145 "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = 0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this has the form $y' + P(x)y = 0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = x^4.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form $y' + P(x)y = Q(x)$ where $Q(x) = x^4$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{3}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form $y' + P(x)y = 0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534562 true] [17592186087538 1100 "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\ny\\sin x\n-\n\\cos x\n=0\n$$" 13194139534562 true] [17592186087538 1100 "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n$\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + y\\sin x - \\cos x =0$" 13194139534562 false] [17592186087538 1145 "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\sin x$ and $Q(x) = \\cos x.$" 13194139534562 true] [17592186087538 1145 "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} + y\\sin x - \\cos x &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} + y\\sin x &= \\cos x \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\underbrace{\\sin x}_{P(x)}y &= \\underbrace{\\cos x}_{Q(x)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\sin x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x) = \\cos x\n$$" 13194139534562 false] [17592186087539 1100 "Determine whether the following equation is a first-order linear ODE:\n\n$$\nx^2\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\n\\sin(2x)y\n=\ne^x.\n$$" 13194139534562 true] [17592186087539 1100 "Determine whether the following equation is a first-order linear ODE:\n\n$x^2\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\sin(2x)y = e^x$." 13194139534562 false] [17592186087539 1145 "We attempt to write the equation in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x) \\,.\n$$\n\nDividing the equation through by $x^2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{2}}y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that this equation is now in standard form with $P(x) = \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{x^2}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{e^x}{x^2}.$\n\nBecause the equation can be written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE." 13194139534562 true] [17592186087539 1145 "We attempt to write the equation in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x) \\,\n$$\n\nOn an interval where $x\\ne0$, dividing the equation through by $x^2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\sin (2x)y &= e^{x} \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{\\sin(2x)}{x^2}y &= \\frac{e^{x}}{x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that this equation is now in standard form with\n\n$$\nP(x) = \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{x^2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x) = \\dfrac{e^x}{x^2}\n$$\n\nBecause the equation can be written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE." 13194139534562 false] [17592186087805 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295528 13194139534562 true] [17592186087805 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295535 13194139534562 true] [17592186087805 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295563 13194139534562 true] [17592186087805 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295619 13194139534562 true] [17592186087806 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295542 13194139534562 true] [17592186087806 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295549 13194139534562 true] [17592186087806 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295556 13194139534562 true] [17592186087806 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295605 13194139534562 true] [17592186087806 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295612 13194139534562 true] [17592186087807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295570 13194139534562 true] [17592186087807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295577 13194139534562 true] [17592186087807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295584 13194139534562 true] [17592186087807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295591 13194139534562 true] [17592186087807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295598 13194139534562 true] [17592186088364 1100 "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x-y}{x}\\,.\n$$" 13194139534562 true] [17592186088364 1100 "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2x - y}{x}$." 13194139534562 false] [17592186088364 1145 "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x}{x}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & +\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{2x}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & +{{\\frac{1}{x}}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y={{2}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=2.$" 13194139534562 true] [17592186088364 1145 "For $x\\ne0$, expand the right side as $y^{\\prime}=2-y/x$. Move the $y$ term to the left to obtain $y^{\\prime}+\\frac1x y=2$. This is standard form with $P(x)=1/x$ and $Q(x)=2$." 13194139534562 false] [17592186295528 1017 #edb/ref 17592186295529 13194139534562 true] [17592186295528 1089 "q-103349" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295528 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534562 true] [17592186295528 1100 "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n{e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{x}y-x=0\n$$" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295528 1113 #uuid "6070c390-c527-5587-a620-4ee028211b12" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295528 1145 "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{x}y-x & =0 \\\\ {e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{x}y & =x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(2{e}^{-x})}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{{e}^{-2x}x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2{e}^{-x}$ and $Q(x)={e}^{-2x}x.$" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295529 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295530 13194139534562 true] [17592186295529 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295531 13194139534562 true] [17592186295529 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295532 13194139534562 true] [17592186295529 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295533 13194139534562 true] [17592186295529 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295534 13194139534562 true] [17592186295529 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534562 true] [17592186295529 1041 "selection" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295529 1104 #edb/ref 17592186295533 13194139534562 true] [17592186295529 1110 #uuid "0894869c-bc84-5110-ae10-0563263bcd72" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295530 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y={e}^{-2x}x" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295530 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534562 true] [17592186295530 1111 #uuid "14ab8be4-03ca-5153-8c0c-7088d7ffafdb" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295531 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y={e}^{-2x}x" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295531 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534562 true] [17592186295531 1111 #uuid "17484bfe-f934-5d3c-9c77-b9ea78107716" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295532 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{-x}y-{e}^{-2x}x=0" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295532 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534562 true] [17592186295532 1111 #uuid "56a46fe8-9518-53fc-bc8b-5e33057c268a" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295533 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{-x}y={e}^{-2x}x" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295533 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534562 true] [17592186295533 1111 #uuid "d284da86-d9f8-5315-b5db-976f864dde0d" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295534 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{-2x}y-{e}^{-x}x=0" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295534 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534562 true] [17592186295534 1111 #uuid "a45c7eaf-12b3-56fe-82d4-fd5314e7b4c6" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295535 1017 #edb/ref 17592186295536 13194139534562 true] [17592186295535 1089 "q-103354" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295535 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534562 true] [17592186295535 1100 "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,y=0\n$$" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295535 1113 #uuid "1de67dfe-a866-57fb-b3b3-9dacfc2c3c42" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295535 1145 "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,y & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,y & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})y & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295536 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295537 13194139534562 true] [17592186295536 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295538 13194139534562 true] [17592186295536 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295539 13194139534562 true] [17592186295536 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295540 13194139534562 true] [17592186295536 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295541 13194139534562 true] [17592186295536 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534562 true] [17592186295536 1041 "selection" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295536 1104 #edb/ref 17592186295539 13194139534562 true] [17592186295536 1110 #uuid "7642a827-a0ad-568b-ba36-f985cbc48b0f" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295537 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295537 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534562 true] [17592186295537 1111 #uuid "cf6a7f40-b2a6-5fd4-9b0f-2e5200133a8e" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295538 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295538 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534562 true] [17592186295538 1111 #uuid "a7a5b571-7dc4-55fc-95b4-794b2cf751c5" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295539 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295539 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534562 true] [17592186295539 1111 #uuid "b73c2a1a-ae93-5d95-a7f2-5a426699abed" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295540 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)y" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295540 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534562 true] [17592186295540 1111 #uuid "2269ae32-16f1-57f5-940e-8b5cbf1d092a" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295541 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295541 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534562 true] [17592186295541 1111 #uuid "982f6313-1aff-5013-864c-7f870615840d" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295542 1017 #edb/ref 17592186295543 13194139534562 true] [17592186295542 1089 "q-103554" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295542 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534562 true] [17592186295542 1100 "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295542 1113 #uuid "a3a7bdfb-b358-530e-a735-49fe9ab8c790" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295542 1145 "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nis already is written in standard form with  $P(x)=-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs." 13194139534562 true] [17592186295543 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295544 13194139534562 true] [17592186295543 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295545 13194139534562 true] [17592186295543 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295546 13194139534562 true] [17592186295543 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295547 13194139534562 true] [17592186295543 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295548 13194139534562 true] [17592186295543 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534562 true] [17592186295543 1041 "selection" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295543 1104 #edb/ref 17592186295544 13194139534562 true] [17592186295543 1110 #uuid "75b6b86d-4093-52ff-a948-ad44ddaf7ebd" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295544 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295544 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534562 true] [17592186295544 1111 #uuid "3f225529-def3-54e2-bfe2-a307d38b0b28" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295545 1034 "{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-{x}^{3}=\\sqrt{1+y}" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295545 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534562 true] [17592186295545 1111 #uuid "58bd1127-2f80-596c-9cc9-160f2ff5de85" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295546 1034 "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295546 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534562 true] [17592186295546 1111 #uuid "f7f51840-b743-5f38-a266-57c6a624f8c0" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295547 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{e}^{xy}=x" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295547 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534562 true] [17592186295547 1111 #uuid "de36a199-47ef-5bf5-b198-80d287b3edb6" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295548 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)={x}^{2}" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295548 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534562 true] [17592186295548 1111 #uuid "fff47300-ef22-525e-a49b-c6e3906d3c77" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295549 1017 #edb/ref 17592186295550 13194139534562 true] [17592186295549 1089 "q-103557" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295549 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534562 true] [17592186295549 1100 "Which of the following is **NOT** a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295549 1113 #uuid "2e2df44b-885f-5606-befe-9f70e9e645a5" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295549 1145 "To d