\n$$" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295563 1113 #uuid "aa9b6eb5-384a-59f1-95c3-f2beb341858b" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295563 1145 "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2} & ={(1-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2} & =1+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx} & =1-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,y & ={{1}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=1.$" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295564 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295565 13194139534562 true] [17592186295564 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295566 13194139534562 true] [17592186295564 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295567 13194139534562 true] [17592186295564 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295568 13194139534562 true] [17592186295564 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295569 13194139534562 true] [17592186295564 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534562 true] [17592186295564 1041 "selection" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295564 1104 #edb/ref 17592186295568 13194139534562 true] [17592186295564 1110 #uuid "76a95351-2164-5d4a-84eb-f7670061af50" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295565 1034 "\\frac{dy}{dx}+y\\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}={(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295565 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534562 true] [17592186295565 1111 #uuid "f1b3e5f7-7eb7-5511-81b1-23c5b6aea1a8" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295566 1034 "\\frac{dy}{dx}=1" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295566 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534562 true] [17592186295566 1111 #uuid "0ed56dab-049e-5c4b-ba10-c8a03243f80d" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295567 1034 "\\frac{dy}{dx}+y\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295567 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534562 true] [17592186295567 1111 #uuid "6a4a410c-9ad1-5027-bb91-c23197893d52" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295568 1034 "\\frac{dy}{dx}+2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295568 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534562 true] [17592186295568 1111 #uuid "a01982d2-9f85-569c-8517-2a0d4aed07ac" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295569 1034 "\\frac{dy}{dx}+4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295569 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534562 true] [17592186295569 1111 #uuid "42d23912-7fe2-56fc-8591-337db7f05398" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295570 1017 #edb/ref 17592186295571 13194139534562 true] [17592186295570 1089 "q-331605" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295570 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534562 true] [17592186295570 1100 "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y$" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295570 1113 #uuid "ba5d1a07-b366-5225-a5ad-9036dc55130b" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295570 1145 "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=3x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation has the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295571 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295572 13194139534562 true] [17592186295571 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295573 13194139534562 true] [17592186295571 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295574 13194139534562 true] [17592186295571 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295575 13194139534562 true] [17592186295571 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295576 13194139534562 true] [17592186295571 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534562 true] [17592186295571 1041 "selection" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295571 1104 #edb/ref 17592186295573 13194139534562 true] [17592186295571 1110 #uuid "4580721f-257e-550b-859d-b876e3e3adc3" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295572 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295572 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534562 true] [17592186295572 1111 #uuid "1219c8ce-b99f-51b9-93cb-8aa2c264a5c7" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295573 1034 "I only" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295573 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534562 true] [17592186295573 1111 #uuid "af3d3b41-1abc-5add-b1ee-6fb3df674df2" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295574 1034 "III only" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295574 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534562 true] [17592186295574 1111 #uuid "d93abfba-138d-5d5c-b1b7-c518978566ad" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295575 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295575 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534562 true] [17592186295575 1111 #uuid "5f4fbc36-6177-5379-b397-43d14ad220a1" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295576 1034 "II only" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295576 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534562 true] [17592186295576 1111 #uuid "e3b9d07b-dd52-5690-9c5a-a6e17e8a5071" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295577 1017 #edb/ref 17592186295578 13194139534562 true] [17592186295577 1089 "q-331725" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295577 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534562 true] [17592186295577 1100 "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=-y$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=-5y$" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295577 1113 #uuid "aa88aa57-e351-5997-9157-8dc2cdacbeb3" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295577 1145 "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=-y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x-1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=-5y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =-5y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy+5y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x-5)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\"" 13194139534562 true] [17592186295578 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295579 13194139534562 true] [17592186295578 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295580 13194139534562 true] [17592186295578 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295581 13194139534562 true] [17592186295578 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295582 13194139534562 true] [17592186295578 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295583 13194139534562 true] [17592186295578 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 131941395345